speechtext:
Mr. Chairman, I appreciate the vigour with which the hon. member has presented his case and with which he is carrying out his assignment. I will cooperate with him as best I can to make sure we get at least to six o'clock before running out of steam on this section of the debate. However, I do not propose to repeat the remarks made during the debate on sections 109 and 110. The debate lasted for several days and dealt at considerable length with the improved tax treatment of parents of disabled or handicapped children. If the hon. member requires the page references in Hansard, I will be glad to send them over.
He is quite correct in his observation that under this section the limit of deduction for such a child would be $500. However, I think that you cannot, in the circumstances to which he alluded, deal only with this section;

one also should refer to the various deductions that are permitted under section 110.
